    "single": {
      "label": "Readings",
      "readings": [
        {
          "role": "first",
          "ref": "Isaiah 64:1-9",
          "refKey": "Isa.64.1-Isa.64.9",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "hope-and-promise",
              "judgment",
              "repentance",
              "sin-and-fall"
            ],
            "image": [
              "fire",
              "mountain"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "lament",
              "longing-and-waiting"
            ],
            "function": [
              "confession",
              "lament-intercession"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 64,
              "v_start": 1,
              "v_end": 1,
              "text": "Rent — A metaphor taken from men, that when they would resolutely help one in distress, break and fling open doors and whatever may hinder. Flow down - That all impediments might be removed out of the way: possibly an allusion to God's coming down upon Mount Sinai, in those terrible flames of fire."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 64,
              "v_start": 2,
              "v_end": 2,
              "text": "Fire — Come with such zeal for thy people, that the solid mountains may be no more before thy breath, than metal that runs, or water that boils by the force of a vehement fire. Known - That thine enemies may know thy power, and that thy name may be dreaded among them."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 64,
              "v_start": 3,
              "v_end": 3,
              "text": "Terrible things — This may relate to what he did among the Egyptians, tho' it be not recorded, and afterward in the wilderness. Looked not for - Such things as we could never expect. Mountains - Kings, princes, and potentates, may metaphorically be understood by these mountains."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 64,
              "v_start": 4,
              "v_end": 4,
              "text": "Besides thee — This is to be applied to all the wonderful works, that God at all times wrought for his people: and thus they are a plea with God, that they might well expect such things from him now, that had done such wonderful things for their fathers. Waiteth - This may be taken with reference both to the state of grace and glory, those incomprehensible things that are exhibited through Christ in the mysteries of the gospel."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 64,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "Meetest — As the father the prodigal. Worketh - That rejoices to work righteousness. Continuance - To those that work righteousness. Be saved - In so doing, in working righteousness."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 64,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "Unclean — Formerly there were some that feared thee; but now we are all as one polluted mass, nothing of good left in us by reason of an universal degeneracy. And all - The very best of us all are no better than the uncleanest things. Taken - Carried away to Babylon, as leaves hurried away by a boisterous wind."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 64,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "That calleth — That call upon thee as they ought. Take hold - Either to stay thee from departing from us, or to fetch thee back when departed."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 64,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "Our father — Notwithstanding all this thou art our father both by creation, and by adoption, therefore pity us thy children."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 64,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "Thy people — Thou hast no people in covenant but us, and wilt thou not leave thyself a people in the world?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "psalm",
          "ref": "Psalm 80:1-7, 17-19",
          "refKey": "Ps.80.1-Ps.80.7",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "deliverance",
              "exile-and-return",
              "prayer-and-worship"
            ],
            "image": [
              "light",
              "shepherd"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "lament",
              "longing-and-waiting"
            ],
            "function": [
              "lament-intercession"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 80,
              "v_start": 1,
              "v_end": 1,
              "text": "Joseph — The children of Joseph or Israel. The name of Joseph, the most eminent of the patriarchs, is elsewhere put for all the tribes. Cherubim - Which were by the mercy seat above the ark."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 80,
              "v_start": 2,
              "v_end": 2,
              "text": "Before Ephraim — Here is an allusion to the ancient situation of the tabernacle in the wilderness, where these tribes were placed on the west-side of the tabernacle, in which the ark was, which consequently was before them."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 80,
              "v_start": 3,
              "v_end": 3,
              "text": "Turn us — To thy self."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "second",
          "ref": "1 Corinthians 1:3-9",
          "refKey": "1Cor.1.3-1Cor.1.9",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "faithfulness",
              "grace",
              "hope-and-promise",
              "last-things"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "assurance",
              "longing-and-waiting"
            ],
            "function": [
              "praise-opening"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 4,
              "v_end": 4,
              "text": "Always — Whenever I mention you to God in prayer."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 5,
              "v_end": 5,
              "text": "In all utterance and knowledge — Of divine things. These gifts the Corinthians particularly admired. Therefore this congratulation naturally tended to soften their spirits, and I make way for the reproofs which follow."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 6,
              "v_end": 6,
              "text": "The testimony of Christ — The gospel. Was confirmed among you - By these gifts attending it. They knew they had received these by the hand of Paul: and this consideration was highly proper, to revive in them their former reverence and affection for their spiritual father."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 7,
              "v_end": 7,
              "text": "Waiting — With earnest desire. For the glorious Revelation of our Lord Jesus Christ - A sure mark of a true or false Christian, to long for, or dread, this Revelation."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 8,
              "v_end": 8,
              "text": "Who will also — if you faithfully apply to him. Confirm you to the end. In the day of Christ - Now it is our day, wherein we are to work out our salvation; then it will be eminently the day of Christ, and of his glory in the saints."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 1,
              "v_start": 9,
              "v_end": 9,
              "text": "God is faithful — To all his promises; and therefore \"to him that hath shall be given.\" By whom ye are called - A pledge of his willingness to save you unto the uttermost."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "role": "gospel",
          "ref": "Mark 13:24-37",
          "refKey": "Mark.13.24-Mark.13.37",
          "tags": {
            "theme": [
              "hope-and-promise",
              "last-things",
              "messianic-hope"
            ],
            "image": [
              "cloud",
              "darkness-and-night",
              "light",
              "seed-and-growth"
            ],
            "mood": [
              "longing-and-waiting",
              "prophetic-warning"
            ]
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 24,
              "v_end": 24,
              "text": "But in those days — Which immediately precede the end of the world: after that tribulation - Above described."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 28,
              "v_end": 28,
              "text": "Matt. xxiv, 32; Luke xxi, 28."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 29,
              "v_end": 29,
              "text": "He is nigh — The Son of man."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 30,
              "v_end": 30,
              "text": "All these things — Relating to the temple and the city."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 32,
              "v_end": 32,
              "text": "Of that day — The day of judgment is often in the Scriptures emphatically called that day. Neither the Son - Not as man: as man he was no more omniscient than omnipresent. But as God he knows all the circumstances of it."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 33,
              "v_end": 33,
              "text": "Matt. xxiv, 42; Luke xxi, 34."
            },
            {
              "chapter": 13,
              "v_start": 34,
              "v_end": 34,
              "text": "The Son of man is as a man taking a far journey — Being about to leave this world and go to the Father, he appoints the services that are to be performed by all his servants, in their several stations. This seems chiefly to respect ministers at the day of judgment: but it may be applied to all men, and to the time of death. Matt. xxv, 14; Luke xix, 12."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
